            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Urdu for to tear (something), then the Urdu for to have (something) put away."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: صفائی",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "صفائی — safāī — \"cleaning; cleanliness\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ہم ہر اتوار کو گھر کی صفائی کرتے ہیں۔ ham har itvār ko ghar kī safāī karte haiṅ. We clean the house every Sunday."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "صفائی (safāī) is built on sāf, \"clean\", which is usually traced, through Persian, to Arabic ṣāfī, \"pure, clear\", with the noun ending -āī."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
